# Trivy exception list for release image scans (scan-images in
# .github/workflows/build-images.yml). See issue #2720 and
# docs/deployment/RELEASE-PIPELINE.md for the Trivy gate this file feeds.
#
# The scan gate is fail-closed: a fixable HIGH/CRITICAL CVE in a release
# image blocks promote. This file is the only sanctioned escape hatch, and
# only for findings that are unfixable upstream or an accepted risk.
#
# The gate reads only this file, never Code Scanning alert state, so
# dismissing an alert in the Security tab does not unblock it (#3231). When
# Security Triage dismisses a release image finding that no entry here or
# open PR already covers, it opens a draft PR on fix/security-<alert-number>
# that adds the entry. Merging that PR is the decision that lets the gate
# pass. Triage never commits to main.
#
# Every entry below must have:
#   - the CVE id, or the GHSA id for an advisory without one
#   - an exp:YYYY-MM-DD expiry on the same line (90 days by default)
#   - a comment explaining why it is unfixable or accepted
#   - a link to the accepted-risk discussion (the Code Scanning alert, or an
#     issue or PR in this repository) that recorded the decision
#
# Put the comments on their own lines directly above the entry, with no blank
# or bare "#" line in between:
#
#   # <package> in the <image> image: <reason>
#   # https://github.com/RackulaLives/Rackula/security/code-scanning/<number>
#   CVE-YYYY-NNNNN exp:YYYY-MM-DD
#
# scripts/check-trivyignore.sh enforces this format before the release scan,
# so an entry without a reason, link, or exp: date fails the gate.
#
# Trivy stops applying an entry after its exp: date, or if the date does not
# parse, so the gate fails on that CVE again until the entry is re-reviewed
# and renewed or removed. Do not add an entry to unblock a release without
# that discussion. If a fix becomes available upstream, remove the entry
# instead of leaving it stale.
#
# No active entries at this time.
